## Questions

### Q1 — Topic 1.1

An insurer wants to “use Claude to generate premium quotes.” Premiums are computed by an
existing actuarial rating engine from three inputs: driver age band, region, and vehicle
class. The bottleneck is that customers email free-text descriptions of themselves and their
vehicles, and agents spend most of their time mapping those emails onto the engine’s inputs.
The business has ruled out changing the intake channel — customers will keep emailing. Which
solution shape best translates this problem?

A) Have Claude compute the premium directly from each email, with the full actuarial table
   in context and worked examples showing correct calculations.

B) Use Claude to extract and normalise the rating inputs from the email, validate them
   against the engine’s allowed values, and pass them to the existing deterministic rating
   engine to compute the premium.

C) Replace the email channel with a structured online quote form, removing the need for a
   language model anywhere in the flow.

D) Have Claude compute the premium and run the rating engine in parallel as a cross-check,
   escalating any mismatch to an agent.

### Q2 — Topic 1.1

A retail bank’s operations lead asks for “a chatbot so branch staff can ask policy
questions.” Discovery shows 80% of questions are about fee-waiver eligibility, which is a
pure function of three fields in core banking. The other 20% are open-ended interpretation
questions across a 400-page policy manual. Leadership wants both question types handled in
the first release, and requires that fee-waiver answers be identical for identical accounts
and never depend on how the question was phrased. Which solution shape fits best?

A) A retrieval-grounded assistant over the full manual that answers every question type
   uniformly, so staff have a single interface.

B) A retrieval-grounded assistant with a tool that queries core banking, so the model can
   run the eligibility check whenever a question calls for it.

C) Ship the deterministic eligibility check first and defer the policy assistant until that
   component has proven its value.

D) A deterministic eligibility check surfaced directly in the staff tool for the fee-waiver
   case, plus a retrieval-grounded assistant for the open-ended policy questions.

### Q3 — Topic 1.1

A freight company asks for “an AI that reads carrier invoices.” Discovery reveals that the
only consumer of the output will be an existing reconciliation job, which matches invoice
lines to shipment records and opens disputes on mismatches. Invoices arrive from 40 carriers
in widely varied layouts. What should the architect define first?

A) The output contract the reconciliation job needs — fields, types, how missing or
   ambiguous values are represented, and which conditions route to a human — then design
   extraction backward from it.

B) A representative corpus of invoices from all 40 carriers, with extraction prompts built
   to capture every field that appears across them.

C) A model bake-off on raw invoice extraction accuracy, so the model tier is fixed before
   any pipeline design begins.

D) A prose-summary prototype that analysts can review, building confidence before committing
   to a structured output format.

### Q4 — Topic 1.1 *(select TWO)*

A hospital group’s goal is to “reduce the time nurses spend on shift handoff.” Which TWO
discovery findings most strongly indicate that an LLM-based component addresses the core
problem, rather than a non-LLM fix?

A) Most handoff delay comes from nurses queuing for a single shared workstation at shift
   change.

B) Handoff information lives mostly in free-text notes written in varied styles, and it must
   be condensed into a consistent structure.

C) Every handoff item already has a structured EHR field that the previous shift reliably
   populates.

D) Safety-critical items (pending medications, fall risk) are mentioned inconsistently, in
   varied phrasing, and scattered across notes.

E) The hospital group has ring-fenced budget for generative AI initiatives this year.

### Q5 — Topic 1.2

A travel-expense assistant extracts merchant, amount, currency, and date from receipt photos
and posts the result to the finance system. Foreign receipts are sometimes posted at face
value in the domestic currency (e.g. ¥12,000 posted as $12,000). The schema has `amount:
number` and a nullable `currency: string`. Traces of the affected cases show `currency`
correctly extracted as “JPY”, “EUR”, and so on in 97% of them. Where does the fix belong?

A) Add few-shot examples of foreign-currency receipts so the currency field is captured more
   reliably.

B) Make `currency` a required field so the model can never omit it.

C) Add an output-stage step between extraction and posting that requires a currency,
   converts non-domestic amounts (or routes null currencies to review), and only then posts.

D) Add an input-stage OCR step that detects currency symbols before the image reaches the
   model.

### Q6 — Topic 1.2

A customer-feedback pipeline sends raw survey comments to Claude for theme classification,
then writes themes and a “representative quote” per theme to an analytics warehouse that 200
staff can access. Legal flags that respondents often include full names and phone numbers in
their comments. That raw text also sits in the pipeline’s trace logs and in the prompts sent
for classification. Where should personal data be handled?

A) Instruct the model to exclude personal details when it selects representative quotes.

B) Restrict warehouse access to a small group of cleared analysts.

C) Add PII detection and redaction at the input stage, before text reaches the model, the
   traces, or any downstream store, so every later stage only ever handles scrubbed text.

D) Add an output filter on the quote field before anything is written to the warehouse.

### Q7 — Topic 1.2

A contract-summary system runs four stages: ingestion → Claude summary → a post-processor
that truncates summaries to 500 characters for a dashboard card → display. Users report that
summaries of long contracts “never mention termination terms.” Offline, the full model
output mentions termination terms in 90% of those contracts, usually near the end of the
summary. What is the most accurate diagnosis?

A) Lost-in-the-middle: the model is under-attending to termination clauses in long
   contracts, so retrieve the relevant sections instead.

B) Prompt failure: the prompt should explicitly require termination terms to be mentioned.

C) Model mismatch: long contracts need a larger model.

D) The output-processing stage is discarding the content. Truncation cuts the tail where
   termination terms appear, so the fix is the output contract (priority items first, or
   structured fields per item), not the model stage.

### Q8 — Topic 1.2

A claims-letter drafting system produces drafts that adjusters edit before sending. The team
wants those edits to improve the system over time without risking regressions. Which
feedback design is most appropriate?

A) Automatically add each final sent letter to the prompt as a few-shot example, so the
   prompt improves continuously.

B) Add thumbs-up/down buttons to each draft and optimise the prompt for approval rate.

C) Capture the draft-vs-sent diff with categorised edit types, sample it into a labelled
   evaluation set, and use that set to test prompt changes before deployment.

D) Periodically fine-tune a model on all sent letters.

### Q9 — Topic 1.4

A due-diligence orchestrator dispatches three worker agents (financial, legal, IP), each of
which reads around 200 documents. Workers return their full exploration transcripts to the
orchestrator, which writes the final memo. Memo quality has degraded, and the memo sometimes
cites working hypotheses that a worker explored and later rejected. What is the best fix?

A) Give the orchestrator a larger context budget so it can hold all three transcripts
   comfortably.

B) Have workers write full transcripts to shared storage and let the orchestrator retrieve
   the relevant portions on demand.

C) Have the orchestrator summarise each transcript before drafting the memo.

D) Define a structured return contract for workers — conclusions, evidence references,
   confidence, open questions — so only concluded results cross the boundary, not the
   exploration trail.

### Q10 — Topic 1.4

A pricing-research system fans out to four agents, each estimating the same competitor’s
price from different sources (list page, reseller feed, sales-call notes, analyst report).
Two agents return $49, one $59, and one $45. The aggregator currently averages them and
reports “$50.50” as fact to the pricing committee. What is the best aggregation design?

A) Report the majority value ($49), since two independent agents agreed.

B) Preserve the disagreement: report each value with its source and date, reconcile using a
   stated source-precedence or recency rule where one exists, and flag unresolved conflicts
   rather than reporting a number no source actually gave.

C) Re-run all four agents and average again to reduce noise.

D) Send the four results to a fifth “judge” agent that selects the most plausible value and
   reports it.

### Q11 — Topic 1.4

An orchestrator dispatches six parallel regional agents to build a global sales report. When
one region’s data API fails, its agent times out and the whole report fails. Regional
managers use their own region’s figures for staffing decisions. The business says a report
covering 5 of 6 regions is useful if the gap is obvious. What is the best design?

A) Bound each worker with a timeout and retry policy, then publish the report with the
   completed regions and an explicit, prominent gap marker for any region that failed.

B) Retry the failed worker indefinitely until it succeeds, so the report is always complete.

C) Fill the missing region with last quarter’s figures so the report stays complete and
   comparable.

D) Run the regions sequentially instead of in parallel so a failure is detected earlier.

### Q12 — Topic 1.4

A support system uses an intake agent that talks to the customer, then hands off to a
billing specialist agent with the message “customer has a billing issue, please help.” The
specialist asks customers to repeat everything, and satisfaction has dropped. Customers
often mention several accounts during intake, and the specialist once acted on the wrong
one. What is the best fix?

A) Pass the full intake conversation transcript to the specialist.

B) Merge intake and billing into a single agent so no handoff is needed.

C) Define a structured handoff payload — verified customer and account identifiers, issue
   summary, steps already taken, the customer’s stated goal, open questions — validated
   before the specialist accepts the handoff.

D) Have the specialist open every handoff with a short set of confirming questions.

### Q13 — Topic 1.5

A compliance team must check 1,200 vendor contracts (around 30 pages each) for the presence
of an indemnity cap and produce a list of contracts missing one. Each contract’s check is
independent of the others, and results are due in three days. What is the best
decomposition?

A) Map-reduce: run the same structured per-contract check independently (in parallel or via
   batch), then aggregate the per-contract results deterministically into the final list.

B) An autonomous agent that works through the contracts, choosing which to examine next
   based on what it has found so far.

C) Pack as many contracts as fit into each request and ask for the missing-cap list per
   batch.

D) An orchestrator agent that plans which contracts need checking and assigns them to
   workers dynamically.

### Q14 — Topic 1.5

A marketing-copy review runs one prompt that performs three independent checks: legal
claims, brand tone, and accessibility language. Reviews are consistently thorough on legal
claims but shallow on accessibility. None of the checks depends on another’s result, and
reviewers wait on the output. What is the best restructuring?

A) Chain the checks sequentially — legal, then tone, then accessibility — passing each
   step’s output into the next.

B) Keep one prompt but move the accessibility criteria to the top.

C) Keep one prompt and add chain-of-thought reasoning across all three checks.

D) Split into three parallel, focused checks (sectioning), each with its own criteria, and
   combine the results.

### Q15 — Topic 1.5

A customer-email pipeline was decomposed into nine sequential LLM calls: detect language,
detect sentiment, detect intent, extract order ID, extract product, draft reply, polish
tone, check policy, translate. End-to-end latency is 14 seconds, and a wrong early
classification is carried through every later step. Evaluations show a single structured
call performs the five detection and extraction steps with equal accuracy. What is the best
change?

A) Parallelise all nine steps to cut latency.

B) Consolidate steps that don’t need independent verification into fewer calls (e.g., one
   structured analysis call), and keep separate steps only where an intermediate check or
   gate adds value.

C) Add a validation call between each of the nine steps to stop errors compounding.

D) Move each of the nine steps to a faster model tier.

### Q16 — Topic 1.5

A team must update every usage of a deprecated internal API across a monorepo of roughly
4,000 files. The number and location of affected usages aren’t known upfront, and some
usages require coordinated changes across several files that must land together. What is the
best decomposition?

A) Fixed parallel sectioning: split the repo into ten equal directory chunks and run one
   worker per chunk.

B) Orchestrator-worker: an orchestrator first discovers the affected usages and their
   cross-file dependencies, then dispatches workers per coherent change unit (grouping
   coupled files), and integrates the results.

C) Map-reduce: one independent call per file decides whether and how to change that file.

D) A single agent session that performs the whole migration sequentially.

### Q17 — Topic 1.6

A telecom deployed Claude on Tier-1 support chat. The project was chartered to fix a
contractual two-minute first-response SLA, which was being breached on 30% of chats and
incurring penalties. The success dashboard now leads with “cost per chat down 40%.” The
sponsor is unconvinced the project is working. What is the core issue?

A) The dashboard measures the cost pillar, but the project was chartered on the
   performance-SLA pillar. It should lead with SLA attainment (share of chats answered
   within two minutes, and the breach-rate trend), with cost as a secondary benefit.

B) Cost per chat is persuasive but needs context, so add an industry benchmark comparison.

C) The dashboard is one-dimensional, so report cost alongside CSAT for a balanced picture.

D) Lead with deflection rate (chats resolved without a human), since it captures both cost
   and speed.

### Q18 — Topic 1.6

An insurer’s small-business underwriting takes five days, driven by manual document review.
With Claude, it will take four hours. Leadership has already approved launching an
instant-quote product for micro-businesses, a segment the insurer couldn’t previously serve
profitably, contingent on this capability. What is the most defensible headline framing for
the business case?

A) Efficiency: review hours per application fall by over 90%.

B) Cost: fewer underwriter hours mean a lower cost per policy.

C) Transformation: the capability enables a product and market the business couldn’t serve
   before, so size the case on that opportunity, with the efficiency gain as supporting
   evidence.

D) Productivity: each underwriter can now handle many more applications.

### Q19 — Topic 1.6 *(select TWO)*

An architect must choose between two first projects. Project X summarises internal meeting
notes for 3,000 staff, with an estimated 15 minutes saved per person per week and no
existing way to measure it. Project Y speeds up supplier-invoice exception handling: an
11-day backlog currently causes $400k a year in late-payment penalties, and exception
volumes and cycle times are already tracked in the ERP. Which TWO factors most strongly
favour Y?

A) X’s larger user base means it would generate more total hours saved.

B) Y’s value is tied to an existing measured baseline (cycle time, penalty spend), so its
   impact can be demonstrated credibly.

C) Y affects fewer users, so change management is simpler.

D) Y would exercise more advanced agentic capabilities.

E) Y’s benefit converts into a hard financial outcome (avoided penalties), while X’s
   scattered time savings may never be recaptured as realised value.

### Q20 — Topic 1.6

A payments firm’s fraud analysts must decide each case within a 90-second regulatory window.
A Claude case summary cut average review time from 70 to 40 seconds. However, p99 model
latency reaches 55 seconds at peak, which pushes some reviews past the window. The business
wants to announce “43% faster reviews” as the headline result. What is the architect’s best
response?

A) Report the average improvement, since it reflects the typical case.

B) Report the median instead, to avoid distortion from outliers.

C) Upgrade to a faster model so the average drops further before announcing.

D) The value pillar here is SLA compliance, which is decided by the tail. Measure and report
   the share of cases decided within 90 seconds, including at peak, and fix the p99 latency
   before claiming the benefit.

