## Answer Key & Rationale

**Q1 — B.** The real problem is unstructured-to-structured mapping, which is where the model
adds value; the calculation is already solved deterministically and should stay that way.
(A: asks the model to reproduce a formula it can only approximate, for no gain. C: would be
right if the channel could change — the stem rules that out. D: pays for a model calculation
whose only job is to be checked by the engine that should have done it in the first place.)

**Q2 — D.** Split the problem by its shape: the deterministic, high-volume case gets a
deterministic path; only the interpretive case gets the model. (A: forces a lookup through
retrieval and generation, with no guarantee of identical answers. B: the closest trap — the
tool itself is deterministic, but whether it gets invoked still depends on the model reading
the phrasing, which violates the “never depend on phrasing” requirement. C: sound
first-project logic in general, but contradicts the stated first-release scope.)

**Q3 — A.** When a system has one known downstream consumer, that consumer’s contract
defines “correct.” Everything upstream is designed to satisfy it. (B: collecting data is
necessary, but “every field that appears” optimises for the source rather than the consumer.
C: model selection needs a defined task and metric first, and the contract is what defines
them. D: a machine consumer can’t use prose, so this prototype validates the wrong output.)

**Q4 — B, D.** Both findings describe unstructured, variably-phrased language that has to be
condensed and normalised, which is the capability an LLM brings. (A: a real cause, but the
fix is hardware or scheduling. C: points to a deterministic report over existing structured
data. E: budget is a funding fact, not evidence of problem–solution fit.)

**Q5 — C.** The traces localise the defect: extraction is right, but the output/posting
stage ignores a field it was given. Fix the stage that is actually failing. (A and D: both
improve an extraction step that is already working. B: the Q2-style trap — a required field
invites fabrication on genuinely ambiguous receipts and still doesn’t make posting honour
the value.)

**Q6 — C.** Personal data flows through three places — the prompts, the traces, and the
warehouse — and only a control at the input stage covers all of them. (D: the strongest
distractor, and correct if the quote field were the only leak, but it leaves the traces and
prompts exposed. A: a soft instruction on one field. B: shrinks the audience without
minimising the data.)

**Q7 — D.** The offline evidence clears the model stage: termination terms are produced and
then removed downstream. Trace the defect to the stage that drops it. (A, B, C: each is a
real diagnosis category from the exam guide, applied to a stage the evidence has already
cleared.)

**Q8 — C.** A good feedback loop turns production signal into a controlled evaluation asset,
so changes are validated rather than absorbed blindly. (A: uncontrolled prompt drift with no
regression check, and it will eventually bloat the context. B: a coarse signal that doesn’t
tell you what was wrong. D: learns from everything, including edits that were one-offs, with
no evaluation gate.)

**Q9 — D.** The worker is the party that knows which hypotheses survived, so the handoff
contract should carry conclusions, not process. (B: progressive retrieval is a real pattern,
but the retrieved portions can still include rejected hypotheses. C: the orchestrator
summarising can’t reliably distinguish a rejected hypothesis from an accepted one. A: more
room for the same noise.)

**Q10 — B.** Aggregating conflicting evidence means keeping provenance and surfacing
conflict. The current design invents a figure that exists in no source. (A: majority voting
assumes the sources are equally reliable, and a stale list page and a fresh sales call
aren’t. D: a judge is a legitimate pattern, but reporting only its pick hides the conflict
from the humans making the decision. C: the disagreement is between sources, not sampling
noise.)

**Q11 — A.** Worker failure should degrade the result gracefully and visibly, not fail it
entirely or hide the gap. (C: a completeness instinct, but managers would staff on figures
that look current and aren’t. B: unbounded retries turn one outage into a missing report. D:
changes when the failure is noticed, not what happens after it.)

**Q12 — C.** A handoff is an interface and should have a contract, and the verified account
identifier is the field that prevents the costly failure. (A: fixes the repetition, but a
transcript mentioning several accounts leaves the specialist to guess which one was
verified. B: reverses the specialisation for one bad interface. D: a safety net that keeps
the repetition customers are already complaining about.)

**Q13 — A.** The work is independent and identical per unit, with a mechanical merge at the
end, which is exactly what map-reduce is for. (C: cheaper-looking, but it reintroduces
long-context recall risk and makes per-contract results unauditable. D: dynamic planning
adds nothing when every contract needs the same check. B: sequential judgement over work
that has no dependencies.)

**Q14 — D.** Independent sub-tasks competing for attention in one prompt call for parallel
sectioning: each check gets full focus, and running them in parallel doesn’t add latency.
(A: decomposes, but invents a dependency that doesn’t exist and triples wall-clock time. B:
may shift which check gets shortchanged rather than fixing the competition. C: more
reasoning within the same shared-attention structure.)

**Q15 — B.** This is over-decomposition: each boundary adds latency and another place for
errors to compound, with no accuracy gain. The eval result shows the granularity is too
fine. (A: the drafting and later steps depend on earlier outputs, so they can’t all run in
parallel. C: treats compounding errors by adding more boundaries. D: every step gets faster,
but the structure stays wrong.)

**Q16 — B.** Unknown scope plus coupled units means the decomposition itself has to be
discovered before work is distributed. That is the orchestrator-worker case. (C: correct for
independent units, as in the contract check, but it breaks here because coupled changes get
split across isolated calls. A: directory boundaries don’t follow dependency boundaries. D:
viable for a small change, but at this scale one context degrades over thousands of files.)

**Q17 — A.** Report value against the pillar the project was funded to move. A real
improvement on a different pillar doesn’t answer the sponsor’s question. (C: balance is a
reasonable instinct, but neither metric is the chartered one. D: deflection is a common
support KPI and still doesn’t measure first-response time. B: sharpens the wrong metric.)

**Q18 — C.** When the capability unlocks a new business model rather than speeding up an
existing one, the headline value is transformation, and sizing only the process saving
understates it. (A, B, D: all true, and all describe the existing process moving faster,
which misses what leadership actually approved.)

**Q19 — B, E.** A first project needs value that is both provable (measured baseline) and
realisable (it converts to an outcome the business actually banks). (A: argues for X, and
rests on an estimate nobody can verify. C: a real but secondary consideration. D: capability
showcase is not a value pillar.)

**Q20 — D.** When the business outcome is a hard deadline, averages and medians hide the
cases that breach it. Measure the pillar at the percentile that decides compliance. (B: the
usual statistical fix, but ignoring outliers is exactly wrong when outliers are the
breaches. C: optimises the average, not the tail. A: headlines a number that doesn’t reflect
the regulatory exposure.)
